soc: sunxi: h713-arisc: standby - crust's request, its budgets and the confirmation on every way out

Contract 5 gives every phase of a standby one guard. crust takes a
request into PRESLEEP, counts its budget there, sleeps, counts the
resuming budget from the wake, and releases on a confirmation for the
same cycle; a budget that runs out ends in its clean reset, and with
the sleep net built its R_WDOG covers a hang. This is the Linux side.

prepare, before anything is quiesced (cpu_comm parks the display MIPS in
its suspend_late, and a standby refused after that leaves no picture):
the firmware runs, is crust of contract 5, is not built with the
VDD_SYS step (Linux restores nothing of the main domain after it) nor
with an unmeasured deep step (PLL LDO, AVCC), has the watchdog link if
it is built with the net, no request is left on the page and crust has
released the last standby (phase off or released, the only phases it
takes a request in); the supervision finds it alive; and one round trip
on port 3 (0x25), which arms nothing while no request is on the page.
Until the 0x11 of this cycle answers, the wake source is none.

suspend_noirq, under the lock now as resume_noirq, in the contract's
order: the last confirmation cleared - a rule of the contract, not
tidiness: one written for a request crust did not take names the cycle
this request asks for again, crust would release it at once, and every
refusal would refuse the next standby too - then the budgets (15 s
before TF-A's request, 60 s from the wake to the confirmation; not
measured), then the request for cycle CYCLE + 1, then the round trip
that hands it over. The request word is cleared on every way out. crust
must answer with phase PRESLEEP, the request taken, the cycle, and with
the net built the net armed; anything else is one error line with the
state, the error bits and the net's read-back, a confirmation for the
cycle, and -EIO. One line per cycle says whether the net is armed and
what the R_WDOG read back.

resume_noirq notes the boot-time clock and sends 0x11 as before. At
PM_POST_SUSPEND, whichever way the suspend ended (a wake, an abort before
TF-A's request, a refusal), a pending request is confirmed, crust gets
100 ms to release, and one line names the cycle, the wake source, the
milliseconds asleep and to the wake, the last step and how long the
release took. crust counts its wakes, so a sleep whose 0x11 brought no
wake word is a wake from an unknown source rather than "did not sleep".
crust released with its net still reading armed - the disarm did not
read back, and it keeps feeding the R_WDOG - is a warning of its own.
Error bits, error counts and TF-A's word - decoded, a denied
SYSTEM_SUSPEND with the checks that failed - are warnings.

TF-A's word is read at probe where it is this boot's: crust of contract
5 on the page, or TF-A's record of this boot, which it writes, filled or
empty, whenever it starts crust - so a crust that never came up is
reported too. A loader clears the word, and TF-A numbers its events from
1 again; the last reported word is forgotten with it, so an event equal
to the last one before the load is not taken for that one.

The PM notifier is registered after the supervision's work, so devm
removes it before the work and before what the start set up. The driver
cannot be unbound through sysfs any more: an unbind during an HDMI call
or a suspend left a consumer with freed memory.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
